From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 16:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: Add the ASUS ProArt PZ14 (HT7407NA)

The ProArt PZ14 (HT7407NA) is a 14 inch OLED tablet on the Snapdragon X2
Elite X2E-88-100, with a keyboard cover on a connector at its lower
edge. Its board is close to the Zenbook A14 (UX3407NA): the PEP power
tables of the two DSDTs are the same apart from the Zenbook's keyboard
I2C bus and one touchscreen pin state, so the tree starts from
glymur-asus-zenbook-a14-ux3407na.dts.

What differs, from this machine's DSDT and the devices Windows
enumerates on it:

- No keyboard, touchpad or keyboard LEDs on I2C or GPIO. The keyboard
  cover is a USB device (0b05:1cd9) on the high-speed only controller,
  so usb_hs is on as a host. The controller's device owns TLMM 9 as an
  output, the reset of the USB 2.0 repeater on the reference board. The
  repeater's I2C address is not in the DSDT, so it is not described,
  its reset is held released and its 3.3 V rail stays on.
- No HDMI port, the third USB controller is off, and Windows does not
  enumerate the multiport controller. So the HDMI bridge, usb_2's PHY,
  mdss_dp2, usb_mp and its two repeaters are gone.
- The touchscreen is a FocalTech FTCS1000 at 0x10 on the same bus and
  interrupt (TLMM 51). Its reset, TLMM 48, is driven high while the
  device is on.
- PCIe 5 is populated, with a Realtek RTS5264 SD Express card reader
  (10ec:5264). Reset, clock request and wake are TLMM 152, 153 and 154,
  and the PHY runs from L2F_E0 and L4H_E0.
- The Wi-Fi and Bluetooth card is a FastConnect C7700 (QCC2072,
  17cb:1112, subsystem 105b:e14f), the card of the Zenbook A16
  (UX3607OA). It is powered the way that board's tree does it.
- The panel is a BOE NB140B9M-T03, which panel-edp identifies by its
  EDID.
- The ADSP and CDSP firmware are this model's, under
  qcom/glymur/ASUSTeK/HT7407NA/.

The lid sensor (TLMM 92), the embedded controller's HID device (0x17 on
i2c10, TLMM 43), the NVMe and Wi-Fi ports and the two USB Type-C ports
are where the Zenbook A14 has them. The interrupts were mapped back
from the GPIO controller's wake interrupt list through the PDC ranges.

The tablet has two speakers and two digital microphones like the
Zenbook A14, so the sound card keeps that machine's name and loads its
topology.

Not described: the volume buttons (PMIC lines the DSDT names by an
index that is not mapped yet), the embedded controller at 0x76 on i2c9,
the two cameras and the sensors behind the ADSP.

Nothing here has run on the tablet yet.

Signed-off-by: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
